{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1487083-the-search-for-an-in-person-opponent","id":1487083,"title":"The Search for an In-Person Opponent","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Additional Discussion","Off-Topic"],"posts":[{"date":"2022-07-19T18:28:49-07:00","id":1487083,"title":"The Search for an In-Person Opponent","content":"Smash me. Degrade my position. Take everything I have and corner me from all directions. What am I talking about? Chess, of course. <br /> <br /> In 2015, the only opponent I played with regularly moved a good distance from me. The main branch of the local chess club was on the other side of the city. I continued to play online intermittently against anonymous players. More recently, I learned a number of openings that were new to me from YouTube, practicing each one at least 100 times in 10m+15s games. I repeatedly won against a chess bot with a rating well above the one I previously had - definitely a confidence booster. <br /> <br /> It is rare enough to find people who know the rules, rarer still people who enjoy the game and exceptionally rare to find an opponent who is around your level (ratings go from 100 to about 3000, where 1000 is baseline). If you find an opponent you can easily beat, but want to keep them interested, you have to avoid the fate of the <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7XqeqzzD0tc\" target=\"_blank\">abominable snowman</a> who drives his friends away. Basically, you have to throw some games to let the person gain confidence and, eventually, the experience they need to pose a real challenge. Even this, for a chess addict, is like animal blood to a vampire. It's a poor substitute for a suitable opponent.<br /> <br /> Today, an acquaintance who had been contemplating getting back into the game after some years sat down and played me across my well worn chessboard. I won the first game by promoting my only remaining passed pawn - very close. On the second, I recognized the opening he went with and intentionally played into it, allowing the full strength of the Sicilian (Dragon Variation) to manifest on the board. He won. Next, I played London System as Black and won. The fourth, I played the Sokolsky, an unconventional open that I haven't mastered. He won. Two-two. <br /> <br /> We had to break and after, he came back and asked to play again. From there, I played familiar opens at full strength and stopped counting win rates. He legit-won some more and we had a good time. I would say that after he dusts off the cobwebs a bit more, he's going to be forcing my best game. I'll be quietly studying more in-depth Chess Tube videos. We resume tomorrow afternoon.<br /> <br /> At long last. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Thank you for passing time with me in conversation. <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1480242-horizon-s-wild-and-crazy-homebrew-rules-and-metaplot-choices\" target=\"_blank\">My Hacks</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26467-horizonparty2021","name":"HorizonParty2021","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26467_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"610"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}